All languages are not run normally, in xPaint, they follow a similar example to PaintScript.
The languages syntaxes are the same, but the way it works is Sprite based.
So KiteScript, and KiteScript Assembly will need to be able to do these things, just lower
lever versions.

########################################
######### PaintScript, Sprite ###########
#########################################

// PaintScript is made to be an easy language,
// Almost like the typeface of JavaScript, with the simple
// functions like in Scratch.

// PaintScript can be applied to two things, a Sprite or a DIItem that interact with
// The DIPlay (DI Player (Display-Integration Player)) (or Diplay if you like).

/*
    It uses three sections for code:
    Code, variables

    Code:
        Can only be used to handle events, like: Start, receive, or KeyPress. Or to define functions.

        Inside of event handlers, any valid syntax can happen, except for WaitUntil on the main Start handler.
        There can only be one start handler, so if you want more than one, make a message and broadcast it
        inside of the start handler. The Start handler is for this sprite only, while messages are universal
        across sprites.

        Variables can only be created here if it's inside a block, like a function, or event handler.
*/

Code:

// Start event

@Start {
    say("Hello, World!"); // Say something (High level, like in Scratch)
    log("Hello, World!") // Output to console
    if (Name == "Nino") {
        log(join("Hello, ", Name, "! Hi!"));
    } else {
        log(join("Hello, " Name, "!"));
    }

    if (exists(message1)) Broadcast message1; // Runs async
    if exists(message2) then BroadcastAndWait message2;
    say(Message);

    // Move the sprite

    center(); // Move the sprite to x = 0, y = 0;

    // Glide to this position in 1 second, to x = 40, y = 40.
    glidePos(1, 40, 40); // Glide to position, use this or glideSprite to glide to a sprite.

    @no-refresh // Run without automatically refreshing the screen
    {
        x = 0;
        y = 0;
    }

    x = 40;
    y = 40;

    // User won't see that the sprite went to 0,0 for a second, because of no-refresh, and then sprite was reset right
    // after.

    // Skins and effects
    switchSkin("Skin-1"); // Switch to skin one, put the name of the skin here.

    var skin = Skin; // Set a variable to the current skin, if the skin is switched, this does not.

    // Interacting with embedded DIItems.
    /*
        Inside of a skin (like a Scratch costume, but with programmable objects inside (Extends DIItem))
        there are programmable objects inside, and can be given instance names (like in Adobe Flash/Animate)
        and be interacted with. It kind of reminds me of JavaScript with the DOM.
    */

    var VideoObject = skin.getVideo("Video-1");
    VideoObject.play();
    var vidLength = VideoObject.length;

    var image = skin.getImage();
    log(image.size);
    log(image); // What will this do?

    // Also the general purpose get
    var object = skin.get("My-object");
    var inner_object = object.get("inner-object");

    // It has the same instance name, but one is inside the other, and the names are not public.
    // Accessed directly only by it's direct parent.
    var inner_inner_object = inner_object.get("inner_object");
    say(inner_inner_object);

    // Calling functions

    // Easy, can be done three ways:
    call myFunction // No Params.
    call myFunction(); // Params.
    myFunction(); // Normal, Params.

    log(myFunction(37)); // Log number.

    // Interaction with other sprites:
    var Sprites = Project.Sprites;
    SpriteList = Sprites.list;
    var Sprite2 = SpriteList["Sprite2"];

    var Sprite3 = Sprites.get("Sprite 3");

    var Sprite4 = skin.getSprite("inner-sprite");

    // You interact like normally, except with the variable name of the sprite first.
    Sprite4.Skin.get("thing or whatever");

    // That's all for now.

}

@receive message1 {
    ("Is done waiting?") = true;
    wait(3000);
    log("Waited 3 seconds!");
}

@receive message2 {
    WaitUntil ("Is done waiting?");
    Message = "Hello!";
}

@KeyPress W, UpArrow, Space {}
@KeyPress A, LeftArrow {}
@KeyPress S, DownArrow {}
@KeyPress D, RightArrow {}

function myFunction(variable) {
    return variable + 1;
}

/*
    In PaintScript, messages are not define, they are simply handled only. If a message is broadcast but
    no receivers? Does nothing, if a receiver is defined, activate any threads that receive that message.
    To broadcast a message, use: Broadcast;
    Messages are async, and are awaited with: BroadcastAndWait.
    If you want to see if a message exists before broadcasting it, use: exists?().
 */

/*
    In PaintScript, it is sometimes easier to manage
    if public (not in a block, public does not mean universal) variables
    are defined after the main code.
    Variables can be defined in block, but also here.
*/
variables:
var Name = "Nino";
var Message = "";
var ("Is done waiting?") = false; // Special variable;

// Exported variables can be used by anything, even other languages, or DIItems.
exported variables:
var SpecialThing = myFunction;


Here is an example of a valid C file in xPaint:

#include <xPaint> // General tools for interacting with xPaint
#include <Sprite> // Import things like x, y, and a sprite definition.
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    Start();
}

// Defined in xPaint.h, function is just void.
function Start() {
    Sprite sprite1 = xPaint.Project.GetThisSprite(); // It does not automatically know, so let it know.
    sprite1.looks.say("Hello, World!");

    printf("Hello, World! \n");

    char* name = "Nino";

    Message message1 = findOrCreate("message1");

    // Because C is not Object Oriented, use PaintScript code in a string.
    message.body = "log"+ name +"\n\
    log("This is from C code!")\n\
    // This is line three.";


    message1.broadcast();

    sprite1.refresh();
}

Here is an example in JavaScript:
// Like in PaintScript, you code, and it goes. Unlike C, you had to get the definition first.

const xPaint = get(); // Default gets standard library.
const Sprite = get("Sprite");

function messageHandler(params) {
    console.log(params);
}

function Start() {
    x = 5;
    y = 5;
    refresh(); // Refresh screen.
    // Add a handler. Same for languages like C#, it will use Actions in C#.
    Messages["message1"].body += messageHandler;

    console.log("Hello, World!");
    log("Hello, World!");
}

// Here is another language, ActionScript

package {
    import xPaint.display.Sprite;
    // import flash.display.Sprite; // Flash version.

    public class Sprite1 extends Sprite {
        public var Name:String = "Nino";

        public function Sprite() {}

        public function Start():void {
            center(); // Move the sprite to x = 0, y = 0;
            refresh(); // Refresh screen.

            function myFunc() {
                trace("Message broadcast!");
                refresh(); // Refresh screen.
                // Glide to position in 1 second, to x = 40, y = 40, pass refresh as a callback for each glide step.
                glideSprite(1, 40, 40, refresh);
                goto(23, 23); // Go to position/Snap to position, instantly go to x = 23, y = 23.
                refresh(); // Refresh screen.

                // Wait until the sprite is at x = 40, y = 40, then run the code.
                while (x == 40 && y == 40) {
                    // Do nothing, wait until the sprite is at x = 40, y = 40.
                }
                
                refresh(); // Refresh screen.
                log("Hello, World!");
                trace("Hello, World! Message done!");
            }

            say("Hello, World!"); // Say something (High level, like in Scratch)
            log("Hello, World!") // Output to console
            trace("Hello, World!") // Output to console, like log, but in ActionScript.

            if (Name == "Nino") {
                log(join("Hello, ", Name, "! Hi!"));
                trace(join("Hello, ", Name, "! Hi!"));
            } else {
                log(join("Hello, " Name, "!"));
                trace(join("Hello, " Name, "!"));
            }

            if (exists(message1)) Broadcast message1;

            Message.addListener(myFunc); // Add a listener for the message, when the message is broadcast, run the function.
        }

        public function MessageListener(message:Message, handler:Function):void {
            Message.addListener(handler);
        }
    }
}



Also here is an example of a valid C# file in xPaint:

using System;
using xPaint;
using xPaint.display;

public class Sprite1 : Sprite {
    public string Name = "Nino";

    public void Start() {
        center(); // Move the sprite to x = 0, y = 0;
        refresh(); // Refresh screen.

        void myFunc() {
            log("Message broadcast!");
            refresh(); // Refresh screen.
            // Glide to position in 1 second, to x = 40, y = 40, pass refresh as a callback for each glide step.
            glideSprite(1, 40, 40, refresh);
            goto(23, 23); // Go to position/Snap to position, instantly go to x = 23, y = 23.
            refresh(); // Refresh screen.

            // Wait until the sprite is at x = 40, y = 40, then run the code.
            while (x == 40 && y == 40) {
                // Do nothing, wait until the sprite is at x = 40, y = 40.
            }
            
            refresh(); // Refresh screen.
            log("Hello, World!");
        }

        say("Hello, World!"); // Say something (High level, like in Scratch)
        log("Hello, World!") // Output to console

        if (Name == "Nino") {
            log(join("Hello, ", Name, "! Hi!"));
        } else {
            log(join("Hello, " Name, "!"));
        }

        if (exists(message1)) Broadcast message1;

        Message.addListener(myFunc); // Add a listener for the message, when the message is broadcast, run the function.
    }

    public void MessageListener(Message message, Action handler) {
        Message.addListener(handler);
    }
}

[#gui] @!! Everything below is GUI code.

[#diplay]
    [#head]
        [#title]KiteScript GUI[/#title]
        [#description]This is a GUI for KiteScript, a programming language for creating games and applications.[/#description]
    [/#head]
    [#body]
        [#h1]KiteScript GUI[/#h1]
        [#p]This is a GUI for KiteScript, a programming language for creating games and applications.[/#p]
        [#button id="runButton"]Run[/#button]
        [#button id="stopButton"]Stop[/#button]
        [#textarea id="codeArea" rows="20" cols="80"][/#textarea]
    [/#body]
[/#diplay]

@!! Does not yet support it's own gui type, but it will in the future. For now, it is html written with KiteScript syntax.

@!! eXtendable KiteScript Markup Language (XKML) is a General Markup Language for general
@!! purpose programming and scripting written with KiteScript Syntax.

[#kxml] @!! A markup language for KiteScript. Every line under this is xkml.

@!! Body

[#data]
    [#table id "Users"]
        @!! Define the pattern for the table header.
        [#pattern id "Header"]
            [#cell]Name:String[/#cell]
            [#cell]Age:Integer[/#cell]
            [#cell]Email:String[/#cell]
        [/#pattern]
        [#row id "User1"]
            [#cell]"John Doe"[/#cell]
            [#cell]30[/#cell]
            [#cell]"john.doe@example.com"[/#cell]
        [/#row]
        [#row id "User2"]
            [#cell]"Jane Smith"[/#cell]
            [#cell]25[/#cell]
            [#cell]"jane.smith@example.com"[/#cell]
        [/#row]
    [/#table]
[/#data]

@!! Normal KiteScript code
[#script]

@!! Blah, blah, blah...

@!! Can use the modifier tags like: [#gui], [#kxml], or [#script] anywhere in the 
@!! file, and it will be treated as that type of code. The modifier tags can be used multiple times in a file.